Grove PATCH-24: give WalletManager a cashaddr scripthash, so a BCH address is watched at all

PATCH-18 recorded, in a comment, that WalletManager._computeScripthash cannot
parse a cashaddr: it runs segwit_addr.decode against _getHRP, and BCH's
chainparams "hrp" is a network-name LABEL, not a bech32 human-readable part --
and cashaddr is not bech32 in any case. It therefore returned "" for every BCH
address. PATCH-18 then worked around that at the one call site it needed
(BCHInterface.importWatchOnlyAddress, which computes a scripthash and passes it
explicitly) and left the function itself as-is.

The two call sites INSIDE wallet_manager.py were not covered by that workaround:

  importWatchOnlyAddress   `if not scripthash: scripthash = self._computeScripthash(...)`
  importAddressWithKey     passes `scripthash=self._computeScripthash(...)` unconditionally

Both stored "" as the watched scripthash for BCH. A watch-only row with an empty
scripthash subscribes to nothing, so the address never reports a balance and its
funding transactions are never observed. importAddressWithKey is how a user's
OWN BCH address is imported, which makes the visible symptom a funded BCH
address showing a zero balance -- and "zero" is indistinguishable from an
address that genuinely has no funds yet, which is why nothing reported it.

This is the half-wired-site shape the 0020/0021/0023 series is named for, at one
more layer: a defect was found, documented precisely, and routed around at the
call site that mattered at the time, leaving the shared function still broken for
every other caller. A comment describing a trap does not disarm it.

  wallet_manager.py :: _bchScripthashForAddress   (new)
      Takes an ADDRESS, not a pubkey hash, and dispatches on the cashaddr's
      encoded type: P2SH20 -> OP_HASH160, P2SH32 -> OP_HASH256, otherwise
      P2PKH. Taking a pubkey hash would have reproduced PATCH-23's bug here --
      hashing a P2PKH script for a P2SH lock address. Scripts are built with
      the engine's own CScript and opcode constants rather than hand-written
      byte literals, so the layout matches BCHInterface.getScriptForPubkeyHash
      and getDestForScriptHash BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by transcription.

  wallet_manager.py :: _computeScripthash
      Branches to the helper for Coins.BCH before the bech32 decode.

  wallet_manager.py :: _deriveAddress
      Now calls the same helper instead of building the P2PKH script a second
      time inline. Two independent implementations of one hash is precisely how
      _computeScripthash came to disagree with _deriveAddress; leaving the
      duplicate in place would have re-armed it.

Found by porting the REU26 research repo's own BCH scripthash harness
(zephyr-testnet/bch-electrum-evidence) into scripts/swap/. That harness compares
FOUR independent scripthash implementations over 16 derived addresses; against
the Grove it reported _computeScripthash returning "" for all 16 while the other
three agreed exactly. The Grove's own offline suite could not have found it: it
was checking the paths PATCH-18 had fixed, not the one it had documented.

verify-bch-electrum.py SECTION 9 asserts the helper agrees with BCHInterface's
own builders for P2PKH, P2SH20 and P2SH32, and that _computeScripthash is
non-empty for BCH -- both with falsification against the pre-patch tree.

Applies on top of PATCH-23. Touches basicswap/, so it counts toward the engine
patch level (p22 -> p23).

diff --git a/basicswap/wallet_manager.py b/basicswap/wallet_manager.py
index e48dab0..23f1515 100644
--- a/basicswap/wallet_manager.py
+++ b/basicswap/wallet_manager.py
@@ -309,11 +309,15 @@ class WalletManager:
             # both shapes as this unit's SECONDARY gate.
             from basicswap.interface.bch.contrib.cashaddress import Address
 
-            script = bytes([0x76, 0xA9, 0x14]) + pkh + bytes([0x88, 0xAC])
-            scripthash = sha256(script)[::-1].hex()
             net = self._swap_client.chain
             version = "P2PKH" if net == "mainnet" else f"P2PKH-{net.upper()}"
             address = Address(version, pkh).cash_address()
+            # PWNDA-PATCH-24: was a second, independent construction of the
+            # same P2PKH script here. Two implementations of one hash is
+            # exactly how _computeScripthash came to disagree with this
+            # method in the first place, so derive it from the address
+            # through the single shared helper instead.
+            scripthash = self._bchScripthashForAddress(address)
             return address, scripthash, pubkey
         # PWNDA-PATCH-19 (Grove-only, BCH account-key glue -- this patch's
         # entire reason to exist): the `if coin_type == Coins.BCH:` branch
@@ -1368,7 +1372,69 @@ class WalletManager:
         # Legacy two-time-pad XOR format.
         return bytes(a ^ b for a, b in zip(encrypted_key, self._getXorKey(coin_type)))
 
+    @staticmethod
+    def _bchScripthashForAddress(address: str) -> str:
+        """Scripthash for a cashaddr, dispatching on its encoded address TYPE.
+
+        PWNDA-PATCH-24. Two things make this necessary rather than tidy:
+
+        1. `_computeScripthash` below could not do it at all. It runs
+           segwit_addr.decode against `_getHRP`, and BCH's chainparams "hrp"
+           is a network-name label, not a bech32 human-readable part --
+           cashaddr is not bech32 to begin with. So it returned "" for every
+           BCH address, and its callers stored that empty string as the
+           watched scripthash. A watch-only row with scripthash "" subscribes
+           to nothing: the address never reports a balance and its funding
+           transactions are never seen. PATCH-18 worked around this at ONE
+           call site (BCHInterface.importWatchOnlyAddress, which passes a
+           scripthash explicitly); the two call sites INSIDE this file --
+           importWatchOnlyAddress's `if not scripthash` default and
+           importAddressWithKey -- kept getting "". The second of those is
+           how a user's own BCH address is imported, so the visible symptom
+           is a funded BCH address showing a zero balance.
+
+        2. It must branch on P2PKH vs P2SH. Hashing a P2PKH script for a P2SH
+           address yields a scripthash for a script that address does not
+           have -- the same defect PATCH-23 fixed in bch.py, and the reason
+           this helper takes an ADDRESS rather than a pubkey hash.
+
+        The scripts are built with the engine's own CScript and opcodes rather
+        than hand-written bytes, so the layout matches
+        BCHInterface.getScriptForPubkeyHash / getDestForScriptHash by
+        construction. verify-bch-electrum.py SECTION 9 asserts that agreement
+        against the interface directly, so a divergence fails rather than
+        drifts.
+        """
+        from basicswap.interface.bch.contrib.cashaddress import Address
+        from basicswap.contrib.test_framework.script import (
+            CScript,
+            OP_CHECKSIG,
+            OP_DUP,
+            OP_EQUAL,
+            OP_EQUALVERIFY,
+            OP_HASH160,
+            OP_HASH256,
+        )
+
+        parsed = Address.from_string(address)
+        payload = bytes(parsed.payload)
+        if parsed.version.startswith("P2SH"):
+            op = OP_HASH256 if len(payload) == 32 else OP_HASH160
+            script = CScript([op, payload, OP_EQUAL])
+        else:
+            script = CScript(
+                [OP_DUP, OP_HASH160, payload, OP_EQUALVERIFY, OP_CHECKSIG]
+            )
+        return sha256(bytes(script))[::-1].hex()
+
     def _computeScripthash(self, coin_type: Coins, address: str) -> str:
+        if coin_type == Coins.BCH:
+            # PWNDA-PATCH-24: see _bchScripthashForAddress above. Without
+            # this branch the decode below returns None and this method
+            # returns "", which its callers store as the watched
+            # scripthash -- silently, and indistinguishably from an
+            # address that simply has no funds yet.
+            return self._bchScripthashForAddress(address)
         _, data = segwit_addr.decode(self._getHRP(coin_type), address)
         if data is None:
             return ""
